2025-11-10 15:53:27,381 - INFO - 第31次尝试，获取到的result_text: {"jobId":33,"status":"complete","data":{"text":"[{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.597\",\"description\":\"This paper investigates the computational hardness of learning Boolean functions from label proportions (LLP), an increasingly relevant machine learning paradigm where only aggregate label information over subsets (bags) of instances is available, generalizing traditional PAC learning. The authors prove several hardness results for LLP: (1) It is NP-hard to find a constant-clause CNF (including ORs) that satisfies any constant-fraction of bags of size at most 2, even when all bags are consistent with an OR function; this contrasts with prior approximation algorithms for halfspaces. (2) It is NP-hard to satisfy more than a (1/2 + δ)-fraction of such bags using constant-size DNF formulas. (3) For parity functions, it is NP-hard to satisfy more than (q / 2^{q-1} + δ)-fraction of the bags of size up to q using a parity function, with the best achievable approximation factor via a randomized algorithm being 1/2^{q-2}. These results show qualitative differences between LLP and PAC learning—the problems are tractable in PAC but intractable in LLP for these classes. The paper uses reductions from known Label Cover problems and applies probabilistic and algebraic techniques to establish tight inapproximability bounds. LLP is motivated by privacy, reduced labeling costs, and applications like medical image analysis. The work highlights fundamental barriers in learning Boolean functions when only label proportions are given, and provides benchmarks for algorithmic progress in LLP.\",\"docName\":\"2403.19401\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:22.834\",\"fileName\":\"2403.19401.pdf\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790421895548928,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Technology\",\"Data Analysis\",\"Research Paper\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"4ff6ba88616fcf7a71d1dca6c9adc14c\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\2403.19401.pdf\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\5a8b97086ee1c90294e728121903432b.pdf\",\"priority\":1,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":14092,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.630\",\"description\":\"A One Day International (ODI) is a limited overs cricket match played between two international teams, typically with each side facing 50 overs. The format is used for high-profile tournaments like the Cricket World Cup and represents the highest standard of List A cricket. ODIs originated in 1971 when a Test match between Australia and England was abandoned due to rain, leading to a one-off game of 40 eight-ball overs, with Australia winning by 5 wickets. Initially, ODIs were played in white kits with a red ball. Significant changes occurred in the late 1970s, driven by Kerry Packer's World Series Cricket, which introduced coloured uniforms, nighttime matches under floodlights, white balls, and enhanced television coverage with multiple cameras and on-screen graphics. The first match with coloured uniforms was held in 1979. These innovations transformed the game, with players becoming full-time professionals, and coloured kits with white balls eventually becoming the ODI standard after 2001.\",\"docName\":\"3nql1cs15r8aviz39pth2bpsr4zvy1\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:22.834\",\"fileName\":\"3nql1cs15r8aviz39pth2bpsr4zvy1.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422033960960,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Sport\",\"Informational\",\"History\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"da0c0f7162b267cca0de596df4c40c56\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3nql1cs15r8aviz39pth2bpsr4zvy1.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\ba50ed51f6b7714754652a80ab62df56.pdf\",\"priority\":2,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":402,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.650\",\"description\":\"The document discusses psychological identity, emphasizing self-image, self-esteem, and individuality. Weinreich defines identity as the totality of one’s self-construal, connecting past, present, and future self-perceptions, and extending this to aspects like ethnic identity. The complexity of describing identity for individuals and groups is highlighted, taking into account both unique characteristics and group memberships, which can vary with circumstance. Drawing on theorists like Kelly, Erikson, and Tajfel, Weinreich’s Identity Structure Analysis (ISA) is introduced as a structural approach to represent individual existential experience. ISA focuses on how relationships between self and others are organized within socio-cultural contexts and over time. It uses constructs from individual, group, and cultural discourses to map the individual's appraisal of self, others, and institutions. The methodology operationalizes these concepts so that identity can be assessed through the situated self’s interactions across different contexts.\",\"docName\":\"3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:26.485\",\"fileName\":\"3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422117847040,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Research Paper\",\"Informational\",\"Science\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"a80f0ad4af9fd250a462680c85e6fbf0\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\044561594142e2ef357c9fbbdfaf4af5.pdf\",\"priority\":3,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":414,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.668\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:25.885\",\"fileName\":\"3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422193344512,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"bb28043dd606535bdcaf1ee30346b0f9\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\c5016b94612aaf89641b2e6895248f70.pdf\",\"priority\":4,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":326,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.688\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:25.902\",\"fileName\":\"AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422277230592,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"b0f396e7936e0f6192cd880927e8e74b\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"priority\":5,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"RUNNING\",\"tokenNum\":0,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.707\",\"descript