56\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3nql1cs15r8aviz39pth2bpsr4zvy1.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\ba50ed51f6b7714754652a80ab62df56.pdf\",\"priority\":2,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":402,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.650\",\"description\":\"The document discusses psychological identity, emphasizing self-image, self-esteem, and individuality. Weinreich defines identity as the totality of one’s self-construal, connecting past, present, and future self-perceptions, and extending this to aspects like ethnic identity. The complexity of describing identity for individuals and groups is highlighted, taking into account both unique characteristics and group memberships, which can vary with circumstance. Drawing on theorists like Kelly, Erikson, and Tajfel, Weinreich’s Identity Structure Analysis (ISA) is introduced as a structural approach to represent individual existential experience. ISA focuses on how relationships between self and others are organized within socio-cultural contexts and over time. It uses constructs from individual, group, and cultural discourses to map the individual's appraisal of self, others, and institutions. The methodology operationalizes these concepts so that identity can be assessed through the situated self’s interactions across different contexts.\",\"docName\":\"3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:26.485\",\"fileName\":\"3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422117847040,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Research Paper\",\"Informational\",\"Science\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"a80f0ad4af9fd250a462680c85e6fbf0\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\044561594142e2ef357c9fbbdfaf4af5.pdf\",\"priority\":3,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":414,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.668\",\"description\":\"In Chapter XVIII, Archie is ordered to assist the Earl and a sizeable group of men-at-arms in searching for Bruce, whose location was revealed by a traitor. Archie, though reluctant, prepares the hound, Hector, for the hunt and considers drastic measures to protect Bruce if they get too close. The party, led by the traitorous peasant, travels five miles into the hills to a hut where Bruce had recently stayed. Upon arrival, Hector becomes energetic and excited, eagerly sniffing around before picking up Bruce's trail. The hound’s pursuit is intense, forcing Archie to struggle to keep pace while the Earl and his knights follow at a slight distance with their men-at-arms. Archie warns the Earl that the noise from the horses and the knights’ equipment agitates the hound further, making him harder to control. The tense search unfolds with Archie contemplating escape should the situation demand it, given the formidable presence of mounted pursuers.\",\"docName\":\"3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:29.649\",\"fileName\":\"3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422193344512,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Informational\",\"History\",\"Report\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"bb28043dd606535bdcaf1ee30346b0f9\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1.txt\",\"previ