ewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\c5016b94612aaf89641b2e6895248f70.pdf\",\"priority\":4,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":326,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.688\",\"description\":\"This document outlines the design of an AI Face Algorithm Evaluation Method, focusing on test set collection and variable matrices. It specifies head movement variables—pitch (up/down), yaw (left/right), and roll (clockwise/counterclockwise)—each with specified movement angles and data rates, as well as facial movements including mouth, eyes, and eyebrows, and associated conditions. Light exposure is detailed into weak, medium, strong, and uneven categories to account for optical flow variability. Horizontal head movement is included, with both left and right motion measured. Data is gathered using a laptop camera and Ruifeng's collection tool, using input video as both the driving video and ground truth; specific images are used as source images. Evaluation involves comparing driving and output videos. The protocol requires 70 videos (minimum 5 seconds each), with 10 videos per variable and targeting multiple participants, with at least 7 represented overall. The data rate defines each video’s contribution per variable. Emphasis is placed on collecting a diverse and representative dataset for robust algorithm assessment.\",\"docName\":\"AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:44.023\",\"fileName\":\"AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422277230592,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Technology\",\"Data Analysis\",\"Specifications\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"b0f396e7936e0f6192cd880927e8e74b\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\c85631aabec673dabeda2b80233b5341.pdf\",\"priority\":5,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":432,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.707\",\"description\":\"This document introduces Linear Programming (LP), outlining its definition, history, methods of solution, and approaches for testing LP solvers. LP involves optimizing a linear objective function based on decision variables subject to linear constraints, creating a convex polyhedral feasible domain where the optimal value is found at domain vertices. Mixed-Integer Programs (MIP) are also defined, and methods like branch-and-bound are discussed. Historically, LP originated with G.B. Dantzig in 1947, with subsequent developments by Kantorovich, Koopmans (who won the Nobel Prize for resource allocation theory), Khachian (Ellipsoid Method), and Karmarkar (Interior-Point Method). Solutions to LP problems use enumeration, Simplex, and Interior-Point methods, with the latter providing polynomial-time solutions for large-scale problems. LP solver testing utilizes both benchmark and self-built test sets, evaluating problems with different constraint and variable structures, including unique feasible, infinite, infeasible, and unbounded cases. Example benchmark instances include AGG, BANDM, BOEING1, and others recorded with details such as rows, columns, nonzeros, bytes, and optimal values. Testing criteria encompass various coefficient scales, constraints, bounds, degeneracy, contradiction, and unboundedness.\",\"docName\":\"A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:58.811\",\"fileName\":\"A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615.pptx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422356922368,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Technology\",\"Data Analysis\",\"Informational\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"0dbb2532524843af54fb3388b7bf3381\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615.pptx\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\236ffb1926cb5ac1cef47fd4707ea1f7.pdf\",\"priority\":6,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":1297,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.764\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:54.916\",\"fileName\":\"Belfast-English.docx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422595997696,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"3193e699ffa92db332ae31a9fb1d3cb4\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\Belfast-English.docx\",\"priority\":7,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"RUNNING\",\"tokenNum\":0,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.784\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.784\",\"errorDescription\":\"\",\"fileName\":\"British-Food-Culture.doc\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422679883776,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"435798f1c3377c7f5b71e18d733ca579\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\British-Food-Culture.doc\",\"priority\":8,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"ADDED\",\"tokenNum\":0,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"20