2025-11-10 15:54:18,984 - INFO - 第55次尝试，获取到的result_text: {"jobId":57,"status":"complete","data":{"text":"[{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.597\",\"description\":\"This paper investigates the computational hardness of learning Boolean functions from label proportions (LLP), an increasingly relevant machine learning paradigm where only aggregate label information over subsets (bags) of instances is available, generalizing traditional PAC learning. The authors prove several hardness results for LLP: (1) It is NP-hard to find a constant-clause CNF (including ORs) that satisfies any constant-fraction of bags of size at most 2, even when all bags are consistent with an OR function; this contrasts with prior approximation algorithms for halfspaces. (2) It is NP-hard to satisfy more than a (1/2 + δ)-fraction of such bags using constant-size DNF formulas. (3) For parity functions, it is NP-hard to satisfy more than (q / 2^{q-1} + δ)-fraction of the bags of size up to q using a parity function, with the best achievable approximation factor via a randomized algorithm being 1/2^{q-2}. These results show qualitative differences between LLP and PAC learning—the problems are tractable in PAC but intractable in LLP for these classes. The paper uses reductions from known Label Cover problems and applies probabilistic and algebraic techniques to establish tight inapproximability bounds. LLP is motivated by privacy, reduced labeling costs, and applications like medical image analysis. The work highlights fundamental barriers in learning Boolean functions when only label proportions are given, and provides benchmarks for algorithmic progress in LLP.\",\"docName\":\"2403.19401\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:22.834\",\"fileName\":\"2403.19401.pdf\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790421895548928,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Technology\",\"Data Analysis\",\"Research Paper\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"4ff6ba88616fcf7a71d1dca6c9adc14c\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\2403.19401.pdf\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\5a8b97086ee1c90294e728121903432b.pdf\",\"priority\":1,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":14092,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.630\",\"description\":\"A One Day International (ODI) is a limited overs cricket match played between two international teams, typically with each side facing 50 overs. The format is used for high-profile tournaments like the Cricket World Cup and represents the highest standard of List A cricket. ODIs originated in 1971 when a Test match between Australia and England was abandoned due to rain, leading to a one-off game of 40 eight-ball overs, with Australia winning by 5 wickets. Initially, ODIs were played in white kits with a red ball. Significant changes occurred in the late 1970s, driven by Kerry Packer's World Series Cricket, which introduced coloured uniforms, nighttime matches under floodlights, white balls, and enhanced television coverage with multiple cameras and on-screen graphics. The first match with coloured uniforms was held in 1979. These innovations transformed the game, with players becoming full-time professionals, and coloured kits with white balls eventually becoming the ODI standard after 2001.\",\"docName\":\"3nql1cs15r8aviz39pth2bpsr4zvy1\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:22.834\",\"fileName\":\"3nql1cs15r8aviz39pth2bpsr4zvy1.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422033960960,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Sport\",\"Informational\",\"History\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"da0c0f7162b267cca0de596df4c40c56\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3nql1cs15r8aviz39pth2bpsr4zvy1.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\ba50ed51f6b7714754652a80ab62df56.pdf\",\"priority\":2,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":402,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.650\",\"description\":\"The document discusses psychological identity, emphasizing self-image, self-esteem, and individuality. Weinreich defines identity as the totality of one’s self-construal, connecting past, present, and future self-perceptions, and extending this to aspects like ethnic identity. The complexity of describing identity for individuals and groups is highlighted, taking into account both unique characteristics and group memberships, which can vary with circumstance. Drawing on theorists like Kelly, Erikson, and Tajfel, Weinreich’s Identity Structure Analysis (ISA) is introduced as a structural approach to represent individual existential experience. ISA focuses on how relationships between self and others are organized within socio-cultural contexts and over time. It uses constructs from individual, group, and cultural discourses to map the individual's appraisal of self, others, and institutions. The methodology operationalizes these concepts so that identity can be assessed through the situated self’s interactions across different contexts.\",\"docName\":\"3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:26.485\",\"fileName\":\"3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422117847040,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Research Paper\",\"Informational\",\"Science\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"a80f0ad4af9fd250a462680c85e6fbf0\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\044561594142e2ef357c9fbbdfaf4af5.pdf\",\"priority\":3,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":414,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.668\",\"description\":\"In Chapter XVIII, Archie is ordered to assist the Earl and a sizeable group of men-at-arms in searching for Bruce, whose location was revealed by a traitor. Archie, though reluctant, prepares the hound, Hector, for the hunt and considers drastic measures to protect Bruce if they get too close. The party, led by the traitorous peasant, travels five miles into the hills to a hut where Bruce had recently stayed. Upon arrival, Hector becomes energetic and excited, eagerly sniffing around before picking up Bruce's trail. The hound’s pursuit is intense, forcing Archie to struggle to keep pace while the Earl and his knights follow at a slight distance with their men-at-arms. Archie warns the Earl that the noise from the horses and the knights’ equipment agitates the hound further, making him harder to control. The tense search unfolds with Archie contemplating escape should the situation demand it, given the formidable presence of mounted pursuers.\",\"docName\":\"3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:29.649\",\"fileName\":\"3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422193344512,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Informational\",\"History\",\"Report\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"bb28043dd606535bdcaf1ee30346b0f9\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\c5016b94612aaf89641b2e6895248f70.pdf\",\"priority\":4,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":326,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.688\",\"description\":\"This document outlines the design of an AI Face Algorithm Evaluation Method, focusing on test set collection and variable matrices. It specifies head movement variables—pitch (up/down), yaw (left/right), and roll (clockwise/counterclockwise)—each with specified movement angles and data rates, as well as facial movements including mouth, eyes, and eyebrows, and associated conditions. Light exposure is detailed into weak, medium, strong, and uneven categories to account for optical flow variability. Horizontal head movement is included, with both left and right motion measured. Data is gathered using a laptop camera and Ruifeng's collection tool, using input video as both the driving video and ground truth; specific images are used as source images. Evaluation involves comparing driving and output videos. The protocol requires 70 videos (minimum 5 seconds each), with 10 videos per variable and targeting multiple participants, with at least 7 represented overall. The data rate defines each video’s contribution per variable. Emphasis is placed on collecting a diverse and representative dataset for robust algorithm assessment.\",\"docName\":\"AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:44.023\",\"fileName\":\"AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422277230592,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Technology\",\"Data Analysis\",\"Specifications\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"b0f396e7936e0f6192cd880927e8e74b\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\c85631aabec673dabeda2b80233b5341.pdf\",\"priority\":5,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":432,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.707\",\"description\":\"This document introduces Linear Programming (LP), outlining its definition, history, methods of solution, and approaches for testing LP solvers. LP involves optimizing a linear objective function based on decision variables subject to linear constraints, creating a convex polyhedral feasible domain where the optimal value is found at domain vertices. Mixed-Integer Programs (MIP) are also defined, and methods like branch-and-bound are discussed. Historically, LP originated with G.B. Dantzig in 1947, with subsequent developments by Kantorovich, Koopmans (who won the Nobel Prize for resource allocation theory), Khachian (Ellipsoid Method), and Karmarkar (Interior-Point Method). Solutions to LP problems use enumeration, Simplex, and Interior-Point methods, with the latter providing polynomial-time solutions for large-scale problems. LP solver testing utilizes both benchmark and self-built test sets, evaluating problems with different constraint and variable structures, including unique feasible, infinite, infeasible, and unbounded cases. Example benchmark instances include AGG, BANDM, BOEING1, and others recorded with details such as rows, columns, nonzeros, bytes, and optimal values. Testing criteria encompass various coefficient scales, constraints, bounds, degeneracy, contradiction, and unboundedness.\",\"docName\":\"A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:53:58.811\",\"fileName\":\"A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615.pptx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422356922368,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Technology\",\"Data Analysis\",\"Informational\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"0dbb2532524843af54fb3388b7bf3381\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615.pptx\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\236ffb1926cb5ac1cef47fd4707ea1f7.pdf\",\"priority\":6,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":1297,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.764\",\"description\":\"This document provides an overview of the historical, social, and linguistic development of Belfast and its English dialect. Founded in 1603, Belfast's population grew significantly in the 19th century due to the Great Famine and industrialisation, notably in linen production and ship-building. The linguistic landscape of Belfast English is shaped by influences from western Scotland and northern England, alongside local features. Sociolinguistic investigations by James and Lesley Milroy from the 1970s onward highlight the importance of social networks, where dense, multiplex connections among working-class groups reinforce distinct vernacular norms, while middle-class, loosely-knit networks are more receptive to standard forms. Studies focus on three specific areas—Ballymacarrett, Hammer, and Clonard—revealing phonological variations tied to network strength, class, and gender. Key features examined include palatalisation, dental realisation of /t/, raising of /a/, and the use of (ʌ). Milroy's work underscores the interplay between standard and vernacular norms and the role of social structure in language variation and change. Extensive references point to historical and sociolinguistic research on Belfast English.\",\"docName\":\"Belfast-English\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:54:14.669\",\"fileName\":\"Belfast-English.docx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422595997696,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Data Analysis\",\"Research Paper\",\"History\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"3193e699ffa92db332ae31a9fb1d3cb4\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\Belfast-English.docx\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\5cade3e91fc5bb369bf72f47d2614c67.pdf\",\"priority\":7,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":2989,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.784\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:54:10.476\",\"fileName\":\"British-Food-Culture.doc\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422679883776,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"435798f1c3377c7f5b71e18d733ca579\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\British-Food-Culture.doc\",\"priority\":8,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"RUNNING\",\"tokenNum\":0,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.803\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T15:52:22.803\",\"errorDescription\":\"\",\"fileName\":\"Netflix Becomes a Broadway Producer With Peter Morgan’s Patriots.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_Bvt_20251110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987790422759575552,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"ab84c46eaa62a18048fb0241446da4ca\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quant